 \begin{abstract}
-I argue that English determinatives, including articles, demonstratives and quantifiers, form a subcategory of Noun alongside common nouns, proper nouns and pronouns. I develop this analysis through comparisons of independent uses, partitives and modification, supported by corpus attestations. Determinatives retain their lexical identity across dependent and independent uses. An explicit set of grammatical rules gives independent determinatives the phrase structure already needed for other nouns, while retaining lexical restrictions on words such as \mention{every} and \mention{the}. Independent determinatives fill ordinary head function instead of jointly filling determiner and head functions. A reanalysis of published data on grammatical properties confirms differences between the coded profiles of determinatives and pronouns. Those differences are compatible with both categories belonging to Noun.
+I argue that English determinatives form a fourth subcategory of Noun, alongside common nouns, proper nouns and pronouns. The argument extends the distributional grounds on which \textit{The Cambridge grammar of the English language} already includes pronouns within Noun despite their distinctive inflection and restricted dependents. Determinatives likewise combine systematic nominal uses with a distinctive internal profile. Comparisons of independent uses, partitives and modification support ordinary nominal headedness while preserving lexical restrictions, including those of articles. An explicit grammatical fragment shows how this analysis extends existing noun-phrase structure and reduces the phrase types admitted in determiner function. Corpus attestations and a reanalysis of published feature data delimit the empirical support. Distributional differences warrant retaining determinative and pronoun as distinct subcategories; they don't by themselves establish a primary-category boundary or decide between coordinate and nested membership within Noun.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} determinatives, nouns, lexical categories, noun phrases, English
