+\subsection{Predicative uses and their restrictions}\label{sec:predication}
+
+Predicative complementation provides a less uniform comparison. \textit{CGEL} records \mention{Its advantages are several} and \mention{Their enemies were many}, describing the latter pattern as uncommon and formal \citep[392, 395--396]{huddleston2002}. Such uses are available to only part of the determinative inventory. The ordinary independent uses of \mention{some} therefore cannot license every determinative in every predicative frame; \mention{every} and the articles retain their restrictions.
+
+The comparison also depends on what the predication expresses. \mention{She is a doctor} ascribes a property, whereas \mention{That is Kim} identifies a person. \textit{CGEL} excludes specifying \mention{be} clauses from its noun--adjective diagnostic because phrases from other categories occur in them too \citep[536]{huddleston2002}. Merely placing an expression after \mention{be} therefore provides no uniform nounhood test.
+
+Restrictions in the opposite direction already occur within nominal grammar. A bare-role NP such as \mention{president} is licensed in \mention{I'd like to be president}, but requires determination in the corresponding object use \mention{I'd like to meet the president} \citep[328]{huddleston2002}. Predicative and argument uses thus need separate conditions within Noun itself. The determinative asymmetries belong in that comparison; they don't establish exclusion merely by departing from a common-noun substitution frame.
+
+\subsection{The cumulative case for ordinary headedness}\label{sec:cumulative}
+
+The comparisons favour ordinary headedness for the simple independent cases. First, nominal argument use recurs across the determinative inventory, so it calls for a category-level account. Second, those expressions are complete without an overt common noun, like ordinary pronoun- and proper-noun-headed NPs. Third, partitives and internally modified expressions locate an overt quantificational head in structures that can share ordinary nominal organization. The modifier and predicative restrictions specify the resulting subcategory's profile.
+
+I take this combined pattern to support extending the existing Noun category. \textit{CGEL} already admits pronouns through shared nominal functions despite distinct inflection and dependents. Determinatives supply a further instance of that relation between common external behaviour and specific internal restrictions. On the D-noun analysis, the ordinary Head relation follows the same nominal sequence across all four subcategories, and lexical identity is preserved across dependent and independent uses.
+
+Fusion can describe the same independent cases, and it remains needed elsewhere. The comparative advantage here is that a widespread use receives the structure already available for other nouns, with the restrictions retained. A separate-D account would earn its additional distinction by capturing a generalization lost under the extension, or by giving the constructions a better analysis. Section~\ref{sec:fragment} identifies what changes when the alternatives receive the same lexical permissions.
 
