 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 
-I argue for determinative as a fourth noun subcategory in English, alongside common noun, proper noun and pronoun. Independent determinatives can share ordinary nominal projection, while their restrictions remain at the subcategory, lexeme and construction levels. The articles remain within determinative on paradigmatic and distributional grounds, with their independent uses explicitly restricted.
-
-The reclassification also removes the DP/NP phrase-type disjunction within determiner function, retaining feature selection and PP determiners. That is a concrete descriptive economy, distinct from replacing determinative fusion with ordinary headedness. The corpus and matrix analyses establish the empirical scope of the comparison. The coordinate classification preserves \textit{CGEL}'s determinative/pronoun distinction; further syntactic generalizations could favour Hudson's nested organization.
+I argue for determinative as a fourth noun subcategory in English. \textit{CGEL} already admits pronouns within Noun on the basis of their phrase functions despite differences in inflection and dependents. Determinatives show the same kind of relation between systematic nominal uses and a distinctive internal profile. The case for inclusion follows that established standard and develops it through independent uses, partitives and modification.
+
+Ordinary headedness gives the simple independent cases the nominal structure already needed elsewhere. Determinatives retain their lexical identity, and the articles retain their paradigmatic membership with independent use restricted. Predicative and modifier differences remain explicit. The analysis also removes the DP/NP phrase-type disjunction within determiner function, preserving feature selection and PP determiners. The cost is a narrower scope for some generalizations previously stated for Noun as a whole.
+
+The corpus and matrix analyses establish the empirical scope of the comparison. They support attested nominal uses and distinguish coded determinative and pronoun profiles without fixing taxonomic rank. Coordinate subcategories preserve that local distinction within the existing nominal architecture; additional shared syntactic generalizations could favour Hudson's nesting. The central proposal is to extend to determinatives the nounhood that the framework already grants to other grammatically distinctive subcategories.
 
